        "In consumer web applications, a combobox dropdown typically contains ten to twenty options: selecting a country, a state, or a billing method. In enterprise applications, however, comboboxes frequently bind to massive industrial datasets: selecting from fifteen thousand equity tickers on a stock exchange, searching ten thousand employee profiles in a multinational directory, or picking from fifty thousand diagnostic codes in an international hospital management portal.",
        "If a design system attempts to render ten thousand `<div role=\"option\">` elements directly into the Document Object Model, the browser immediately buckles under the weight. Initial DOM node creation consumes hundreds of megabytes of memory, virtual DOM tree diffing stalls the main thread for several seconds, and scrolling the popup drops frames from sixty hertz down to two hertz, creating an unusable, freeze-prone experience. The solution to this challenge is Virtual Windowing (DOM Virtualization).",
        "Virtualization operates on a simple mathematical principle: at any given moment, the user's physical display can only show twenty or thirty options inside the scrollable popup window (the Viewport). Rendering the other 9,970 off-screen options is a complete waste of CPU cycles and memory. A virtualized list renders only the exact slice of items that currently intersect the visible viewport, plus a small buffer of two or three items above and below (the Overscan Buffer) to ensure smooth scrolling without white flashes.",
